<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anagni</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Anagnia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anagni, ancient Anagnia, is a hill town in the Sacco valley south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and was the leading city of the Hernici. Rome punished it after its revolt of 306 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a favourite residence of the popes, and in 1303 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a> was seized there by agents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anagni.jpg" alt="Anagni" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LPLT · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piazza_cavour_Anagni.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Anagni is a hill town of about 424 metres above sea level in the province of Frosinone, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, east-southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It keeps the look of a small medieval town, with twisting streets and steep lanes inside Roman boundary walls. The first people known by name in the area were the Hernici, said to have come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aniene">Aniene</a> valley, and Anagni was an important city and spiritual centre of theirs, built on an acropolis and partly defended by walls of almost squared stone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 307 BCE the Hernici, except for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alatri">Alatri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veroli">Veroli</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferentino">Ferentino</a>, declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and, after setbacks, surrendered. In 306 BCE Anagni and the other states that had borne arms were admitted to citizenship without the vote, forbidden councils and intermarriage, and left only magistrates in charge of religious rites. Several emperors later spent their summers there, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commodus">Commodus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a>.</p><p>Anagni has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat since the 5th century. From the 10th and 11th centuries the popes regarded it as a safer and healthier place than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and it became one of their favourite residences. In 1160 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a> excommunicated Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> in the cathedral, and on 29 September 1227 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> excommunicated Emperor Frederick II there. The town produced four popes in a century, three of them of the Conti family, among them Innocent III.</p><p>In 1303 Guillaume de Nogaret, adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a>, and Sciarra Colonna led about two thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> against the palaces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a>. The pope was held for three days without food or drink until the townspeople rose and freed him. This is called the Outrage of Anagni, and a later legend says Colonna slapped the pope with his gauntlet. Boniface died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 11 October 1303. The move of the papal court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> began a long decline, and Duke Werner von Urslingen sacked the town in 1348.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Romanesque cathedral of Saint Mary, built in 1071–1105 with Gothic additions in the mid-13th century, has frescoes among the best preserved examples of Romanesque and Byzantine art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and an unrestored Cosmati floor. The Communal Palace was built in 1163 by the Brescian architect Jacopo da Iseo. Excavations since 2006 at the imperial Villa Magna, south of the town, have revealed a large, richly decorated building devoted to wine production.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anagni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pope Boniface VIII, James II of Aragon, Philip IV of France, Charles II of Naples and James II of Majorca made an accord at <b>Anagni</b> confirming the Treaty of Tarascon of 1291.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-20">20 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Nicola Boccasini was elected pope as Benedict XI, succeeding Boniface VIII, who had died weeks earlier, shortly after his seizure at <b>Anagni</b> in his conflict with Philip IV of France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-22">22 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After Pope Boniface VIII excommunicated Philip IV of France, the king's troops, led by his councillor Guillaume de Nogaret, attacked the pope's residence at <b>Anagni</b> and captured him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-7">7 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>They were an Italic people, probably related to the Sabellians, organised by the 5th century BCE in a league of towns centred on <b>Anagnia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hernici">Hernici</a></li></ul></section>
